$schema: https://usm.dev/schema/v1.json
$id: usm/cli-multi-lang-scan
$type: feature
$version: 1
$last_updated: "2026-08-28"
status: built
summary: >-
  Multi-language scanner support, extended with a detector plugin system. usm scan detects services,
  frameworks, routes, data models, and infrastructure from 12+ languages and 30+ frameworks via
  built-in detectors. Users and agents extend detection through two declarative surfaces — a
  detection section in usmconfig.json and auto-discovered .usm/detectors/*.yaml files — with a
  script escape hatch for convention-based frameworks. The scanner is generalized: service detection
  is driven by detector manifests, removing the package.json hardcode, so non-JS/TS stacks (Go, Zig,
  etc.) are first-class. Existing Node.js/TypeScript scanning is unchanged.
$system: usm/system
$service: usm/cli
intent: >-
  The .usm format, MCP tools, generators, and validator are all language-agnostic, but the scanner
  only detects Node.js/TypeScript projects (package.json, Next.js routes, Prisma schemas) plus
  hardcoded Python/Docker/Prisma/Terraform special-cases. This feature extends the scanner to detect
  services and routes from common frameworks across all major programming languages AND makes the
  detection itself extensible without an upstream PR — users drop a .usm/detectors/zig.yaml file or
  add a detection entry to usmconfig.json, and usm scan picks it up. A script escape hatch handles
  convention-based frameworks (Next.js file-routing) that regex cannot express. The generalized
  orchestrator replaces the package.json hardcode so any detector manifest (go.mod, build.zig.zon,
  etc.) drives service detection.
decisions:
  - id: manifest-based-detection
    decision: Detect services by language manifest files
    rationale: |
      Every language ecosystem has a standard manifest file. Detecting these
      files is reliable, language-specific, and doesn't require parsing source
      code. The manifest also tells us dependencies (framework detection).
    consequences: Scanner needs a manifest-to-language map. New languages added by adding a manifest entry.
    status: accepted
  - id: framework-specific-route-detection
    decision: Detect routes per-framework, not per-language
    rationale: |
      Route patterns differ by framework, not language. Next.js uses app/page.tsx,
      FastAPI uses @app.get decorators, Spring uses @GetMapping annotations,
      Rails uses config/routes.rb, ASP.NET uses [HttpGet] attributes.
      Each framework needs its own route extractor.
    alternatives:
      - option: Generic AST parsing per language
        rejected_because: Too complex, requires language-specific parsers, fragile
      - option: File-pattern matching only (no framework awareness)
        rejected_because: Misses decorator/annotation-based routes (FastAPI, Spring, ASP.NET)
    consequences: Route detection is framework-by-framework. New frameworks need a route extractor.
    status: accepted
  - id: configurable-in-usmconfig
    decision: Language/framework detection rules are configurable in usmconfig.json
    rationale: |
      Users can add custom manifest patterns, route patterns, and data model
      patterns for frameworks we don't support out of the box. Defaults cover
      common frameworks; advanced users can extend.
    consequences: usmconfig.json gains a 'detection' section with language/framework rules
    status: accepted
  - id: all-languages-from-start
    decision: Support all major languages from the initial implementation
    rationale: |
      Rather than phasing, include all major languages (Python, Go, Rust, Java,
      Kotlin, C#, Ruby, PHP, Elixir, Swift, Scala, C/C++) from the start. The
      manifest detection is simple (file pattern matching) and route detection
      is regex-based per framework. The complexity is manageable.
    consequences: Larger initial implementation but no phased rollout complexity
    status: accepted
  - id: detectors-directory
    decision: >-
      Add .usm/detectors/*.yaml as a second auto-discovered extension surface alongside
      usmconfig.json detection
    rationale: >-
      usmconfig.json describes a single repo's shape and is per-project. Detector files are
      shareable across repos, versioned with the .usm source of truth, validatable like other USM
      artifacts, and agent-writable via MCP tools. Two surfaces with the same field shapes give
      users the cheap path (drop a file) and the config path (inline rules) without forcing one or
      the other.
    alternatives:
      - option: usmconfig.json detection only (original accepted decision)
        rejected_because: >-
          Config is per-project and not shareable; cannot express convention-based frameworks that
          need code
      - option: .usm/detectors/ only, drop the config section
        rejected_because: Reverses an accepted decision and removes the inline config ergonomics for small overrides
    consequences: >-
      Two surfaces to keep in sync; precedence rules must be deterministic. Built-in detectors
      migrate to the same shape as user detectors.
    status: accepted
  - id: script-escape-hatch
    decision: >-
      Allow routes.script in a detector to point at a .ts/.js file exporting
      extractRoutes(sourceDir, framework) for convention-based frameworks
    rationale: >-
      Regex cannot capture Next.js app/page.tsx convention routing, Remix, or SvelteKit file-based
      routes. These need real logic. A script field keeps the declarative detector file as the entry
      point but delegates extraction to code when needed. The script lives in the user's repo (not
      auto-loaded from third parties), bounding the trust surface.
    consequences: >-
      Detector files can reference local code; USM dynamically imports it. Only opt-in per detector;
      declarative regex remains the default.
    status: accepted
  - id: generalized-orchestrator
    decision: >-
      Generalize structural.ts service detection so any detector manifest drives it, removing the
      package.json hardcode
    rationale: >-
      Today a service directory without package.json is warned and skipped, making non-JS/TS stacks
      second-class. The whole point of multi-language scan is that a Go app (go.mod) or Zig app
      (build.zig.zon) is detected as a service without package.json. The orchestrator must ask
      detectors 'does your manifest match this directory' instead of assuming package.json.
    consequences: >-
      The Python pyproject.toml and Docker docker-compose special-case passes become built-in
      detectors. structural.ts shrinks to an orchestrator that iterates detectors.
    status: accepted
  - id: infrastructure-as-detector
    decision: >-
      Treat infrastructure (Terraform today) as a detector kind rather than a hardcoded separate
      subcommand
    rationale: >-
      infrastructure.ts only parses Terraform. Users with CloudFormation, Pulumi, or CDK get
      nothing. An infrastructure detector kind with the same manifest+pattern shape as other
      detectors makes IaC extensible without code, consistent with the rest of the plugin system.
    consequences: >-
      usm scan infrastructure subcommand continues to work (backed by the built-in Terraform
      detector); new formats add via detector files or detection.infrastructure in config.
    status: accepted
  - id: precedence-order
    decision: >-
      Detector precedence is built-in defaults < .usm/detectors/ files < usmconfig.json detection
      (last wins)
    rationale: >-
      Built-ins provide sane defaults out of the box. Detector files are project-level
      customizations shared across the team. usmconfig.json is the highest precedence so a user can
      override a detector file from config without editing the file (useful for one-off overrides).
      Deterministic order prevents merge ambiguity.
    consequences: >-
      Documented precedence; overrides by $id for detectors and by manifest pattern for config
      arrays.
    status: accepted
flows:
  - id: detect-multi-lang-services
    name: Detect services from multiple language manifests
    description: |
      Scanner reads usmconfig.json detection.manifests (or defaults) and checks
      for manifest files. Each manifest identifies a service and its language.
      Framework is detected from dependencies in the manifest.
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: read
        target: usmconfig.json detection.manifests (or defaults)
      - id: s2
        action: scan
        target: directories for all manifest files
      - id: s3
        action: parse
        target: each manifest for framework dependencies
      - id: s4
        action: classify
        target: service type (web-app, api, worker) from framework
      - id: s5
        action: generate
        target: .usm/services/<name>.usm with detected language, runtime, framework
  - id: detect-python-routes
    name: Detect Python routes (FastAPI, Flask, Django)
    description: |
      Scan .py files for decorator-based routes. FastAPI uses @app.get/@router.get,
      Flask uses @app.route, Django uses urlpatterns in urls.py.
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: scan
        target: .py files in service directory
      - id: s2
        action: match
        target: >-
          FastAPI: @app.(get|post|put|delete|patch), @router.(get|post|...); Flask: @app.route;
          Django: path('', views)
      - id: s3
        action: extract
        target: path and HTTP method from decorator/pattern
      - id: s4
        action: generate
        target: routes[] in feature .usm files
  - id: detect-go-routes
    name: Detect Go routes (chi, gin, echo, net/http)
    description: |
      Scan .go files for router registration patterns. chi/gin/echo use
      r.GET/r.POST, net/http uses http.HandleFunc, gorilla/mux uses r.HandleFunc.
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: scan
        target: .go files in service directory
      - id: s2
        action: match
        target: (r|router|mux)\.(GET|POST|PUT|DELETE|PATCH|HandleFunc|Handle)
      - id: s3
        action: extract
        target: path and HTTP method
      - id: s4
        action: generate
        target: routes[] in feature .usm files
  - id: detect-rust-routes
    name: Detect Rust routes (Axum, Actix, Rocket)
    description: |
      Scan .rs files for route patterns. Axum uses .route(), Actix uses
      #[get(...)] macros, Rocket uses #[get("/path")] attributes.
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: scan
        target: .rs files in service directory
      - id: s2
        action: match
        target: ".route(), .service(), #[get(...)], #[post(...)], #[route(GET, \"/path\")]"
      - id: s3
        action: extract
        target: path and HTTP method
      - id: s4
        action: generate
        target: routes[] in feature .usm files
  - id: detect-java-routes
    name: Detect Java/Kotlin routes (Spring Boot, Javalin, Quarkus)
    description: |
      Scan .java/.kt files for annotation-based routes. Spring uses
      @GetMapping/@PostMapping/@RequestMapping, Javalin uses app.get/post,
      Quarkus uses @Path + @GET/@POST.
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: scan
        target: .java and .kt files in service directory
      - id: s2
        action: match
        target: >-
          Spring: @GetMapping/@PostMapping/@RequestMapping; Javalin: app.get/post; Quarkus:
          @GET/@POST + @Path
      - id: s3
        action: extract
        target: path and HTTP method from annotation
      - id: s4
        action: generate
        target: routes[] in feature .usm files
  - id: detect-csharp-routes
    name: Detect C# routes (ASP.NET Core, Minimal APIs)
    description: |
      Scan .cs files for attribute-based routes. ASP.NET uses [HttpGet],
      [HttpPost], [Route], Minimal APIs use app.MapGet/MapPost.
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: scan
        target: .cs files in service directory
      - id: s2
        action: match
        target: "[HttpGet], [HttpPost], [Route(\"path\")], app.MapGet, app.MapPost"
      - id: s3
        action: extract
        target: path and HTTP method from attribute
      - id: s4
        action: generate
        target: routes[] in feature .usm files
  - id: detect-ruby-routes
    name: Detect Ruby routes (Rails, Sinatra)
    description: |
      Scan config/routes.rb for Rails routes (get/post/resources) and .rb
      files for Sinatra routes (get '/path' do).
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: scan
        target: config/routes.rb and .rb files in service directory
      - id: s2
        action: match
        target: "Rails: get/post/resources/namespace; Sinatra: get '/path' do, post '/path' do"
      - id: s3
        action: extract
        target: path and HTTP method
      - id: s4
        action: generate
        target: routes[] in feature .usm files
  - id: detect-php-routes
    name: Detect PHP routes (Laravel, Symfony, Slim)
    description: |
      Scan routes/web.php for Laravel routes (Route::get/post), .php files
      for Symfony attributes (#[Route]) and Slim ($app->get/post).
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: scan
        target: routes/web.php, routes/api.php, and .php files
      - id: s2
        action: match
        target: "Laravel: Route::get/post; Symfony: #[Route]; Slim: $app->get/post"
      - id: s3
        action: extract
        target: path and HTTP method
      - id: s4
        action: generate
        target: routes[] in feature .usm files
  - id: detect-elixir-routes
    name: Detect Elixir routes (Phoenix)
    description: |
      Scan router.ex for Phoenix routes (get/post/pipe_through/scope).
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: scan
        target: lib/*_web/router.ex files
      - id: s2
        action: match
        target: get/post/put/patch/delete within scope blocks
      - id: s3
        action: extract
        target: path and HTTP method
      - id: s4
        action: generate
        target: routes[] in feature .usm files
  - id: detect-swift-routes
    name: Detect Swift routes (Vapor)
    description: |
      Scan .swift files for Vapor route registrations (routes.get/post,
      app.get/post).
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: scan
        target: .swift files in service directory
      - id: s2
        action: match
        target: routes.get/post/put/delete, app.get/post
      - id: s3
        action: extract
        target: path and HTTP method
      - id: s4
        action: generate
        target: routes[] in feature .usm files
  - id: detect-scala-routes
    name: Detect Scala routes (Akka HTTP, Play, Tapir)
    description: |
      Scan .scala files for route patterns. Akka HTTP uses path/endpoints,
      Play uses routes file, Tapir uses endpoint.get/post.
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: scan
        target: .scala files and conf/routes files
      - id: s2
        action: match
        target: "Akka: path/endpoints; Play: GET /path; Tapir: endpoint.get/post"
      - id: s3
        action: extract
        target: path and HTTP method
      - id: s4
        action: generate
        target: routes[] in feature .usm files
  - id: detect-cpp-routes
    name: Detect C++ routes (Crow, Drogon, Pistache)
    description: |
      Scan .cpp/.h files for route registration patterns. Crow uses
      CROW_ROUTE, Drogon uses app.registerHandler, Pistache uses
      router.get/post.
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: scan
        target: .cpp and .h files in service directory
      - id: s2
        action: match
        target: "Crow: CROW_ROUTE; Drogon: registerHandler; Pistache: router.get/post"
      - id: s3
        action: extract
        target: path and HTTP method
      - id: s4
        action: generate
        target: routes[] in feature .usm files
  - id: detect-multi-lang-data-models
    name: Detect data models from multiple ORMs across languages
    description: |
      Extend data model detection beyond Prisma to support ORMs across
      all supported languages.
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: scan
        target: known ORM schema files per language
      - id: s2
        action: parse
        target: model definitions (class-based, struct-based, macro-based, annotation-based)
      - id: s3
        action: generate
        target: .usm/data/<name>.usm with models and fields
  - id: discover-detector-files
    name: Discover and load detector files
    description: >-
      At scan start, the orchestrator reads .usm/detectors/*.yaml (if present), validates each
      against detector-v1.json, and merges them with built-in defaults and the usmconfig.json
      detection section. Precedence: built-in defaults < .usm/detectors/ files < usmconfig.json
      detection (last wins).
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: read
        target: .usm/detectors/*.yaml if directory exists
      - id: s2
        action: validate
        target: each detector file against detector-v1.json schema
      - id: s3
        action: merge
        target: built-in defaults + detector files + usmconfig.json detection (config overrides files)
      - id: s4
        action: warn
        target: on invalid detector files (skip, do not abort scan)
  - id: detect-service-via-detector
    name: Detect a service from any language manifest
    description: >-
      Replaces the hardcoded package.json read. For each directory matched by a services rule, the
      orchestrator finds the detector whose manifest glob matches a file in that directory and reads
      that manifest (not always package.json). A Go app with go.mod is detected via the Go detector;
      a Zig app with build.zig.zon via the Zig detector. No package.json is required.
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: match
        target: directory against usmconfig.json services rules
      - id: s2
        action: find
        target: detector whose manifest glob matches a file in the directory
      - id: s3
        action: parse
        target: that manifest file (package.json, go.mod, build.zig.zon, etc.)
      - id: s4
        action: classify
        target: language, runtime, framework from manifest contents
      - id: s5
        action: generate
        target: .usm/apps/<name>/service.usm
  - id: extract-routes-via-script
    name: Extract routes for convention-based frameworks via script
    description: >-
      Frameworks whose routes come from file conventions (Next.js app/page.tsx, Remix, SvelteKit)
      rather than declarations cannot be captured by regex. A detector declares routes.script
      pointing at a .ts file exporting extractRoutes(sourceDir, framework). The orchestrator imports
      and runs it. Declarative regex patterns remain the default; script is opt-in per detector.
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: read
        target: detector routes.script path
      - id: s2
        action: import
        target: the script module dynamically
      - id: s3
        action: call
        target: extractRoutes(sourceDir, framework)
      - id: s4
        action: generate
        target: routes[] in feature .usm files
  - id: detect-infrastructure-via-detector
    name: Detect infrastructure from IaC files via detectors
    description: >-
      Generalizes the Terraform-only infrastructure scan. An infrastructure detector declares a
      manifest glob (infrastructure/**/*.tf, **/cloudformation.yaml, **/*.pulumi.ts) and resource
      extraction patterns. Built-in Terraform detector preserves current behaviour; users add
      CloudFormation/Pulumi/CDK via detector files or usmconfig.json detection.infrastructure.
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: match
        target: infrastructure detector manifest globs
      - id: s2
        action: parse
        target: matched IaC files for resources
      - id: s3
        action: emit
        target: infrastructure YAML block per service
  - id: detect-data-schema-via-detector
    name: Detect data models from any ORM via detectors
    description: >-
      Generalizes the Prisma-only data scan. A data-schema detector declares a manifest glob and
      model extraction patterns. Built-in Prisma detector preserves current behaviour; users add
      SQLAlchemy, Diesel, GORM, Entity Framework via detector files or usmconfig.json
      detection.data_models.
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: match
        target: data-schema detector manifest globs
      - id: s2
        action: parse
        target: model definitions (class, struct, macro, annotation)
      - id: s3
        action: generate
        target: .usm/packages/<pkg>/<orm>.usm with models[]
contracts:
  - id: manifest-detection-coverage
    description: Scanner detects services from all supported language manifests
    must_have:
      - package.json → Node.js/TypeScript/JavaScript
      - pyproject.toml or requirements.txt → Python
      - Cargo.toml → Rust
      - go.mod → Go
      - pom.xml or build.gradle → Java/Kotlin
      - .csproj or .sln → C#/.NET
      - Gemfile → Ruby
      - composer.json → PHP
      - mix.exs → Elixir
      - Package.swift → Swift
      - build.sbt → Scala
      - CMakeLists.txt or Makefile → C/C++
      - Framework detected from dependencies in manifest
  - id: route-detection-coverage
    description: Scanner detects routes from 30+ frameworks across all languages
    must_have:
      - "Next.js: app/page.tsx, app/route.ts (existing)"
      - "Express: app.get/post/put/delete in .js/.ts files"
      - "FastAPI: @app.get/post decorators in .py files"
      - "Flask: @app.route decorators in .py files"
      - "Django: path() patterns in urls.py"
      - "Go chi/gin/echo: r.GET/POST and router.HandleFunc patterns"
      - "Go net/http: http.HandleFunc patterns"
      - "Rust Axum: .route() calls"
      - "Rust Actix: #[get/post] macros"
      - "Rust Rocket: #[get/post] attributes"
      - "Spring Boot: @GetMapping/@PostMapping/@RequestMapping annotations"
      - "Javalin: app.get/post calls"
      - "Quarkus: @GET/@POST + @Path annotations"
      - "ASP.NET Core: [HttpGet]/[HttpPost] attributes"
      - "ASP.NET Minimal: app.MapGet/MapPost"
      - "Rails: get/post/resources in config/routes.rb"
      - "Sinatra: get '/path' do in .rb files"
      - "Laravel: Route::get/post in routes/web.php"
      - "Symfony: #[Route] attributes"
      - "Slim: $app->get/post in .php files"
      - "Phoenix: get/post in router.ex"
      - "Vapor: routes.get/post in .swift files"
      - "Akka HTTP: path/endpoints in .scala files"
      - "Play: GET /path in conf/routes"
      - "Tapir: endpoint.get/post in .scala files"
      - "Crow: CROW_ROUTE macro in .cpp files"
      - "Drogon: registerHandler in .cpp files"
      - "Pistache: router.get/post in .cpp files"
  - id: data-model-detection-coverage
    description: Scanner detects data models from ORMs across languages
    must_have:
      - "Prisma: schema.prisma (existing, TypeScript)"
      - "SQLAlchemy: class definitions in models.py (Python)"
      - "Django ORM: class definitions in models.py (Python)"
      - "GORM: struct definitions with gorm tags (Go)"
      - "Diesel: table! macros in schema.rs (Rust)"
      - "Hibernate: @Entity annotations in .java (Java)"
      - "Entity Framework: DbSet properties in DbContext (C#)"
      - "ActiveRecord: class definitions inheriting ApplicationRecord (Ruby)"
      - "Eloquent: class definitions extending Model (PHP)"
      - "Ecto: schema definitions in .ex files (Elixir)"
  - id: config-extensible
    description: Detection rules are configurable in usmconfig.json
    must_have:
      - detection.manifests array with {pattern, language, frameworks?} objects
      - detection.routes array with {framework, pattern, method_group, path_group} objects
      - detection.data_models array with {orm, pattern, model_pattern?} objects
      - Defaults cover all supported frameworks; users can add custom patterns
  - id: backward-compatible
    description: Existing Node.js/TypeScript scanning unchanged
    must_have:
      - usm scan on a Node.js project produces same results as before
      - usmconfig.json without detection section uses defaults
  - id: detector-file-format
    description: >-
      Detector files in .usm/detectors/*.yaml are validated against a detector-v1.json schema before
      use
    must_have:
      - >-
        Each detector declares $id, kind (service|framework|routes|data-schema|infrastructure),
        manifest glob, language, runtime
      - Framework detectors declare frameworks[] with name + string-contains detect rule
      - >-
        Route detectors declare routes.extensions + routes.patterns[] (regex, methodGroup,
        pathGroup) OR routes.script (mutually exclusive)
      - Invalid detector files are skipped with a warning, not an abort
  - id: dual-extension-surfaces
    description: >-
      Users can extend detection through either usmconfig.json or .usm/detectors/ files, with the
      same expressiveness
    must_have:
      - >-
        usmconfig.json detection.manifests / detection.routes / detection.data_models /
        detection.infrastructure arrays (existing accepted decision, unchanged)
      - .usm/detectors/*.yaml files as a second, auto-discovered source
      - >-
        Both surfaces accept the same field shapes (manifest, frameworks, routes patterns, data
        model patterns)
      - >-
        Detector files support routes.script for convention-based frameworks; usmconfig.json
        detection.routes supports script too
  - id: orchestrator-generalized
    description: >-
      Service detection is driven by detector manifests, removing the package.json hardcode in
      structural.ts
    must_have:
      - >-
        A service directory is detected if ANY detector manifest glob matches a file in it (not only
        package.json)
      - >-
        A Go app with go.mod and no package.json produces a service.usm (no 'No package.json found'
        warning)
      - A Zig app with build.zig.zon and no package.json produces a service.usm
      - >-
        The existing Python pyproject.toml and Docker docker-compose passes become built-in
        detectors, not special-case code
  - id: infrastructure-extensible
    description: Infrastructure detection is extensible beyond Terraform via detectors
    must_have:
      - Built-in Terraform detector preserves usm scan infrastructure output exactly
      - detection.infrastructure array in usmconfig.json (new, mirrors detection.manifests shape)
      - Infrastructure detector files accepted in .usm/detectors/
      - CloudFormation, Pulumi, CDK detectable via user-supplied detectors without code change
  - id: precedence-and-override
    description: Detector sources merge with a deterministic precedence so users can override built-ins
    must_have:
      - >-
        Built-in detectors are the lowest precedence (seeded from current
        MANIFESTS/ROUTE_PATTERNS/Prisma/Terraform tables)
      - .usm/detectors/ files override built-ins by detector $id
      - >-
        usmconfig.json detection section is highest precedence (overrides both built-ins and
        detector files)
      - >-
        A usmconfig.json with no detection section and no .usm/detectors/ dir produces identical
        results to the current scanner (backward compatible)
  - id: backward-compatible-existing-implementations
    description: Existing USM projects with usmconfig.json and .usm files continue to scan identically
    must_have:
      - >-
        usm scan on a Node.js/Next.js project with no detection section and no detectors dir
        produces the same .usm output as before this feature
      - Existing PRESERVE_FIELDS / UPDATE_FIELDS smart-merge behaviour unchanged
      - Existing CLI flags (--force, --routes, --merge) unchanged in name and default
      - >-
        The existing manifest-detection-coverage and route-detection-coverage contracts from the
        original spec remain satisfied by the migrated built-in detectors
tests:
  - id: detect-python-service
    setup:
      pyproject_toml_with_fastapi: true
    expect:
      - assertion: ".usm/services/<name>.usm created with runtime: python"
      - assertion: "framework: fastapi detected"
  - id: detect-go-service
    setup:
      go_mod_with_gin: true
    expect:
      - assertion: ".usm/services/<name>.usm created with runtime: go"
      - assertion: "framework: gin detected"
  - id: detect-rust-service
    setup:
      cargo_toml_with_axum: true
    expect:
      - assertion: ".usm/services/<name>.usm created with runtime: rust"
      - assertion: "framework: axum detected"
  - id: detect-java-service
    setup:
      pom_xml_with_spring_boot: true
    expect:
      - assertion: ".usm/services/<name>.usm created with runtime: java"
      - assertion: "framework: spring-boot detected"
  - id: detect-csharp-service
    setup:
      csproj_with_aspnet: true
    expect:
      - assertion: ".usm/services/<name>.usm created with runtime: dotnet"
      - assertion: "framework: aspnet-core detected"
  - id: detect-ruby-service
    setup:
      gemfile_with_rails: true
    expect:
      - assertion: ".usm/services/<name>.usm created with runtime: ruby"
      - assertion: "framework: rails detected"
  - id: detect-php-service
    setup:
      composer_json_with_laravel: true
    expect:
      - assertion: ".usm/services/<name>.usm created with runtime: php"
      - assertion: "framework: laravel detected"
  - id: detect-elixir-service
    setup:
      mix_exs_with_phoenix: true
    expect:
      - assertion: ".usm/services/<name>.usm created with runtime: elixir"
      - assertion: "framework: phoenix detected"
  - id: detect-swift-service
    setup:
      package_swift_with_vapor: true
    expect:
      - assertion: ".usm/services/<name>.usm created with runtime: swift"
      - assertion: "framework: vapor detected"
  - id: detect-scala-service
    setup:
      build_sbt_with_akka: true
    expect:
      - assertion: ".usm/services/<name>.usm created with runtime: scala"
      - assertion: "framework: akka-http detected"
  - id: detect-cpp-service
    setup:
      cmake_with_drogon: true
    expect:
      - assertion: ".usm/services/<name>.usm created with runtime: cpp"
      - assertion: "framework: drogon detected"
  - id: extract-fastapi-routes
    setup:
      python_file_with_decorators: true
    expect:
      - assertion: routes[] extracted with correct paths and methods
  - id: extract-spring-routes
    setup:
      java_file_with_annotations: true
    expect:
      - assertion: routes[] extracted from @GetMapping/@PostMapping
  - id: extract-aspnet-routes
    setup:
      cs_file_with_attributes: true
    expect:
      - assertion: routes[] extracted from [HttpGet]/[HttpPost]
  - id: extract-rails-routes
    setup:
      routes_rb_with_get_post: true
    expect:
      - assertion: routes[] extracted from get/post/resources
  - id: extract-laravel-routes
    setup:
      web_php_with_route_get: true
    expect:
      - assertion: routes[] extracted from Route::get/post
  - id: extract-phoenix-routes
    setup:
      router_ex_with_get_post: true
    expect:
      - assertion: routes[] extracted from get/post in scope blocks
  - id: backward-compatible-node
    setup:
      package_json_with_nextjs: true
    expect:
      - assertion: same results as before this feature
  - id: custom-detection-rules
    setup:
      usmconfig_with_custom_manifest: true
    expect:
      - assertion: custom manifest pattern detected
  - id: detector-file-zig-detected
    setup:
      usm_detectors_zig_yaml: declares manifest build.zig.zon, framework zap, route regex
      apps_zig_api_build_zig_zon_with_zap: true
    expect:
      - assertion: ".usm/apps/zig-api/service.usm created with runtime: zig, framework: zap"
      - assertion: no 'No package.json found' warning
      - assertion: routes[] extracted from .zig source files
  - id: infrastructure-detector-cloudformation
    setup:
      usm_detectors_cloudformation_yaml: declares manifest **/cloudformation.yaml, resource extraction pattern
      infrastructure_cloudformation_yaml_with_resources: true
    expect:
      - assertion: infrastructure YAML block emitted for CloudFormation resources
  - id: script-route-extraction-nextjs
    setup:
      usm_detectors_nextjs_yaml: "declares routes.script: ./detectors/nextjs.ts"
      apps_web_app_page_tsx: true
    expect:
      - assertion: routes[] extracted by the script, matching the current Next.js route extraction output
  - id: config-overrides-detector-file
    setup:
      usm_detectors_go_yaml: declares framework gin detect 'gin'
      usmconfig_detection_manifests: overrides go framework detect to 'echo'
    expect:
      - assertion: scan uses the usmconfig.json detection rule (echo), not the detector file (gin)
  - id: no-package-json-service-detected
    setup:
      apps_go_api_go_mod_with_gin: true
      no_package_json: true
    expect:
      - assertion: ".usm/apps/go-api/service.usm created with runtime: go, framework: gin"
      - assertion: no warning emitted
  - id: backward-compatible-no-detectors-dir
    setup:
      package_json_with_nextjs: true
      no_usm_detectors_dir: true
      usmconfig_without_detection_section: true
    expect:
      - assertion: scan output identical to pre-feature scanner (same services, routes, features)
  - id: invalid-detector-file-skipped
    setup:
      usm_detectors_bad_yaml: malformed YAML or missing required fields
    expect:
      - assertion: scan continues, warning emitted naming the invalid file
      - assertion: other detectors still load and run
implementation:
  primary: src/scan/detectors.ts
  test_code: tests/detectors.test.ts
  test_code_status: manual
see_also:
  - usm/cli-scan
  - usm/cli-init
